WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.468 align:start size:84% position:16%
- When we were
dominating the debate

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:06.806 align:start position:0%
on critical race theory in late
2020, and then really 2021,

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:09.142 align:start size:88% position:13%
you saw the left retreat
from these issues.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:11.845 align:start position:0%
All of a sudden, Robin DiAngelo
was nowhere to be found.

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:15.181 align:start size:88% position:13%
Ibram Kendi was you
know, on the defensive.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:18.017
And then you saw this
shift in the debate

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.286 align:start size:84% position:16%
when those same
teachers and districts

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:23.256 align:start size:97% position:3%
that had been pushing
critical race theory in 2021,

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:26.559 align:start size:94% position:6%
started really doubling
down on, especially trans,

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:29.462
non-binary and queer
identity formations

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:33.400
as the new thing, the new
trend, the new you know,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.636 align:start size:88% position:13%
current thing, with
capital C and capital T.

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they thought that they
could really just shift

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.741
the debate and win
on these grounds.

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:45.045 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so in a sense,
they're complimentary.

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:48.615
They can play three-card
monte with these two ideas.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:50.884
But I think there's
also a deeper nexus

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.286
or connection between the two.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:56.756
You know, students at the
elementary school level,

00:00:56.756 --> 00:01:01.160
high school level, you
know, they can be shamed.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.265
They can be told that
their racial category,

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:06.766
you know, let's say for example,

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.601
white students are told that
they have white privilege,

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:12.272 align:start size:97% position:3%
white fragility, unconscious
white racial superiority.

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:15.642 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're part of the
systemically racist society

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:18.778
by virtue of their ancestry.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:22.115
But that doesn't really, in
some ways, help tremendously.

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:23.483
You can't change your race.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:26.052
They can't do anything about it

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:28.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
at the level of
their own identity.

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:33.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
But these sexual
identities offer an escape

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:36.028 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the guilt and
shame that was instilled

00:01:36.028 --> 00:01:38.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the critical race
theory narrative.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.001
And they offer an escape,
so you get, in some cases,

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.903
you know, I talk to teachers
that say, "yeah," you know,

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:47.307
"40% of my white female
students in middle school

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:51.978
now identify as non-binary,
transgender, et cetera."

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:55.081
And so it gives them an
avenue for participation,

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:57.083 align:start size:81% position:19%
not just at the
level of ideology,

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.185 align:start size:81% position:19%
but the deeper
level of identity.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:01.788
And so in some ways,
critical race theory

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.991
serves to push people into
some of these constructs

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:08.595
as an avenue out.
(upbeat music)

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.101
- I am Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:18.237 align:start size:88% position:13%
is a senior fellow at
the Manhattan Institute,

00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:20.440
and author of the
brand new book,

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.975
"America's Cultural Revolution:

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.845
How the Radical Left
Conquered Everything."

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:29.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
Chris Rufo, my friend, welcome
back to "The Rubin Report."

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:31.951
- It's great to be with you.

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:34.754
- Chris, did they
conquer everything?

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:36.089
Everything Chris?

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:38.758
- Yeah, I mean, obviously,

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:41.027
there's a little bit
of fun and hyperbole.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.795
Certainly, they haven't
conquered your studio

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
or my studio, even though
(Dave laughs)

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
they might be banging down
the door at any minute.

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:48.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, yeah.
- But in all seriousness,

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:50.203
they really did.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:52.438
I mean, I think that's what
we really learned in 2020.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
All of a sudden people
looked around and they saw

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:57.343 align:start size:97% position:3%
that all of their institutions
had been conquered

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:58.811
by the ideologies of the left,

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:01.781 align:start position:0%
whether it's critical race
theory, radical gender ideology,

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:04.316
so-called diversity,
equity, and inclusion.

00:03:04.316 --> 00:03:07.120 align:start size:97% position:3%
All of our private
companies were really riding

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.254
that BLM line.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.156 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so many Americans
looked around

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:11.658
and said, well, what happened?

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:13.726
How did this suddenly occur?

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:16.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
That all of these
neo-Marxist ideologies

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.530
seemed to be at the forefront
of all of our institutions?

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:22.068 align:start position:0%
And the reason I wrote this
book was to answer that question

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.970
and to dig deep
into the history,

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.638 align:start size:84% position:16%
going back more
than a half century,

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:28.207
explaining how this happened,

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:30.143 align:start size:84% position:16%
what is going on
with our institutions

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:31.811
and how we can fight back.

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.812
- All right, so I wanna
dive into all of that.

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:36.282
And clearly, my audience
knows an awful lot about it

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:37.984
and has tracked you
for quite some time.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:39.285
You've been on the
show a million times.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
But for somebody
that's watching this,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.223 align:start size:88% position:13%
that is not too
familiar with your work,

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:45.158 align:start size:88% position:13%
how did you get involved
in this whole thing?

00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:48.494
Because you've become
sort of the center

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:50.797
of fighting back against this.

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.499
- That's right, it
really all started

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:56.569
with my investigative reporting
for "City Journal" magazine.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:00.873 align:start position:0%
I looked at some training
programs in the summer of 2020.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:03.309
Sources around the country
were sending me documents

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:05.011
from their DEI programs.

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.779
Talking about systemic racism,

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:09.148
white privilege,
white fragility,

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.651
segregating employees
on the basis of race

00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:13.452
and shaming some employees

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:16.689 align:start size:97% position:3%
and praising other employees
based on their ancestry.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:21.260 align:start position:0%
And this reporting really
exploded in the season of 2020.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.262
And I identified
that the ideology

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:24.864
that was driving this
kind of programming

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.866
was critical race theory.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.268
I went on "Tucker," when
he was still on Fox News,

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:33.906
and asked President Trump
to sign an executive order

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:36.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
abolishing CRT trainings
from the Federal Government.

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:38.778 align:start size:84% position:16%
He did so, and that
really launched

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:42.515 align:start size:97% position:3%
this whole line of reporting,
this line of activism,

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:46.585
and really, this line of
inquiry to say, you know,

00:04:46.585 --> 00:04:49.088 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a democracy, we think
that majority opinion

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:51.724
should be translated in
our public institutions.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:54.060 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's not the
case, for example,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.995 align:start size:84% position:16%
when schools are
pushing CRT on kids,

00:04:56.995 --> 00:05:00.966 align:start position:0%
over and against the best wishes
and the desires of parents.

00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:04.303
And so while I made my
name, in a certain sense,

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:07.339
as an activist, as a
muckraking reporter,

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:09.641
what I wanted to do was
also give those readers

00:05:09.641 --> 00:05:12.745
a chance to go deeper, a
chance to really understand

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.346
what's happening
under the surface,

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:17.116 align:start size:84% position:16%
what is the genealogy
of these ideas.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:21.387 align:start size:88% position:13%
And really, go beyond
the quick hit, you know,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.723
headlines and news bites,

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:25.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
and giving something
very substantive

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:27.993
so that those of us on the right

00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:30.630
or in the center or
even the center-left,

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.298
can be armed with all the
information that we need

00:05:33.298 --> 00:05:37.136 align:start size:97% position:3%
in order to start fighting
back against these ideologies,

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:38.471
both at an intellectual level,

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:40.740
but also at a political level.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.009
- Alright, so let's do some
101 for some of the people

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:45.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are just catching
up on this stuff.

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:49.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
Can you define
critical race theory?

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:52.985 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Sure, critical race theory
is an academic discipline

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:55.655
that holds that the United
States is a fundamentally

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:57.856
and systemically racist country.

00:05:57.856 --> 00:06:00.693
And that all of our
institutions, from
our legal system

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:04.831
to our constitution, even to
the Civil Rights Act itself,

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:07.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
are smoke screens
that preach the values

00:06:07.833 --> 00:06:11.436 align:start size:88% position:13%
of liberty and freedom,
but in reality,

00:06:11.436 --> 00:06:15.541
serve to uphold a system
of naked racial domination.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.711
And so it's this idea that
any disparity in outcomes

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:21.647
can be traced back
to a hidden, a secret

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:24.517
or an invisible racist system.

00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:26.218 align:start size:84% position:16%
That the critical
race theorists believe

00:06:26.218 --> 00:06:30.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're privileged in
their ability to ferret out

00:06:30.223 --> 00:06:32.024
all of these hidden racisms.

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:34.227 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so critical
race theory though,

00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:37.330
is the ideology at
the tip of the spear,

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.433
but it traces back
decades upon decades,

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:42.968
back to neo-Marxist ideology,

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:45.571
that's really the
very basis of this.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:49.375
And so while critical race
theory was hotly debated

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:52.144
in the headlines for
a number of years,

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:56.281
we have to understand that in
order to really root it out,

00:06:56.281 --> 00:06:59.351
we have to go even a
further step deeper.

00:06:59.351 --> 00:07:02.088
- So when we had that
like, three month blip,

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.490 align:start size:84% position:16%
probably about a
year and a half ago,

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
where everybody in
mainstream media kept saying,

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:10.596 align:start size:97% position:3%
"oh, they don't teach critical
race theory in schools.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:11.798
They don't teach it in schools."

00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:14.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
And yet every day,
those of us that live,

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
at least partially online,
we would see, you know,

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:18.704 align:start size:84% position:16%
curriculum that
was getting leaked.

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:21.106
We'd see teachers, you
know, caught on the DL,

00:07:21.106 --> 00:07:23.142
admitting they're teaching
it, et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:23.142 --> 00:07:24.543
What was that like for you?

00:07:24.543 --> 00:07:26.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because it wasn't necessarily
that they were handing

00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:28.848 align:start size:84% position:16%
a book to the
children that said,

00:07:28.848 --> 00:07:30.349
this is critical race theory,

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:33.118
but they were figuring out
other ways to leak the ideas

00:07:33.118 --> 00:07:36.556 align:start size:88% position:13%
that you just expressed,
into all of the stuff

00:07:36.556 --> 00:07:38.057
that they've been teaching.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:39.591
- Yeah, I mean, it
was unbelievable

00:07:39.591 --> 00:07:41.326 align:start size:88% position:13%
because I was
publishing these stories

00:07:41.326 --> 00:07:42.962 align:start size:84% position:16%
with leaked
curriculum documents.

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.798
I was always posting the
original source materials,

00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:48.501
sometimes hundreds of
pages of materials.

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:51.304 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then even in
states like California,

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:52.938
Washington, and Illinois,

00:07:52.938 --> 00:07:55.541
they were putting critical
race theory, explicitly,

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.576 align:start size:88% position:13%
in their state-mandated
curriculum.

00:07:57.576 --> 00:08:00.679
But for months, the left
said critical race theory

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:05.584 align:start position:0%
doesn't exist, it's a boogeyman,
it's a kind of phenomenon

00:08:05.584 --> 00:08:08.086 align:start size:88% position:13%
of a fevered
right-wing imagination.

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:10.122
And in all of these
pieces attacking me,

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:13.726
they never once addressed the
specifics of my reporting,

00:08:13.726 --> 00:08:15.861
where I put it in very
clear, black and white,

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:17.663
this is what they're teaching,

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:19.866
these are the principles
of critical race theory.

00:08:19.866 --> 00:08:22.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is the intention
of these lessons.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:26.005
And so they tried to maintain
that posture for a long time,

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:27.506
that critical race
theory doesn't exist.

00:08:27.506 --> 00:08:30.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
But what happened is that
we hit them every day

00:08:30.342 --> 00:08:34.713
with more facts, more evidence,
more concrete documentation.

00:08:34.713 --> 00:08:37.115 align:start size:88% position:13%
So that position slowly
became untenable.

00:08:37.115 --> 00:08:40.286
And even the more honest
and writers and journalists

00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:42.521
on the left started to
say, well, you know,

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:44.823
critical race theory
is in the curriculum.

00:08:44.823 --> 00:08:47.092
Maybe it's not labeled
critical race theory,

00:08:47.092 --> 00:08:50.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
but certainly the
principles are at play here.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:54.366
And so there was a kind of
concession for a little bit.

00:08:54.366 --> 00:08:57.403 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then it flipped, following
the really natural script

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:58.704
that we've seen over and over.

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:00.106
Then they started saying, yeah,

00:09:00.106 --> 00:09:02.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
there is critical
race theory and good,

00:09:02.707 --> 00:09:04.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
we want critical race
theory in the curriculum.

00:09:04.543 --> 00:09:06.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you saw even the
largest teachers union

00:09:06.479 --> 00:09:09.047 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the United States
pass a resolution saying

00:09:09.047 --> 00:09:11.183
that we want to have
critical race theory

00:09:11.183 --> 00:09:15.054
in all 14,000 local
school districts.

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:17.456
You know, they said it
very explicitly by name,

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:20.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
we love CRT, we
want CRT everywhere.

00:09:20.058 --> 00:09:24.062 align:start position:0%
And then the media started
defending critical race theory.

00:09:24.062 --> 00:09:26.198 align:start size:97% position:3%
But there was one thing
that was really interesting,

00:09:26.198 --> 00:09:29.535 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that parents, throughout
this whole debate,

00:09:29.535 --> 00:09:32.104
throughout this whole subterfuge

00:09:32.104 --> 00:09:35.007
and really, manipulation
on behalf of the media,

00:09:35.007 --> 00:09:36.508
they knew what was happening.

00:09:36.508 --> 00:09:38.444
And that's why they showed
up at school board meetings.

00:09:38.444 --> 00:09:40.378
That's why they were protesting,

00:09:40.378 --> 00:09:42.414 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's why they were demanding
changes to the curriculum

00:09:42.414 --> 00:09:45.351
because they saw not
just my reporting,

00:09:45.351 --> 00:09:47.319
but what was happening in
their local communities,

00:09:47.319 --> 00:09:49.421
what kids were bringing
home in their backpacks

00:09:49.421 --> 00:09:50.756
every day after school.

00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:52.257
And they said, wait a minute.

00:09:52.257 --> 00:09:55.127 align:start position:0%
We do not want racial
scapegoating in our curriculum.

00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:57.029
We do not want our
kids being judged

00:09:57.029 --> 00:09:59.431
as avatars of racial categories,

00:09:59.431 --> 00:10:01.968
and we do not want to be
taught a false history

00:10:01.968 --> 00:10:03.535 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the United States
that condemns it

00:10:03.535 --> 00:10:06.605
as a racist society
through and through.

00:10:06.605 --> 00:10:11.009
And so you know, that was
the arc of the debate.

00:10:11.009 --> 00:10:13.646 align:start position:0%
And I think ultimately, whether
you look at public opinion,

00:10:13.646 --> 00:10:14.947
whether you look at legislation,

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:17.049
whether you look
at even the posture

00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:20.186 align:start size:97% position:3%
of our opponents on the left,
I think we resoundingly,

00:10:20.186 --> 00:10:22.888
won that debate over
the course of that year

00:10:22.888 --> 00:10:24.423
or two-year period.

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:26.425
- Can you talk about
how this is connected

00:10:26.425 --> 00:10:29.895
to all of the gender stuff,
so it's not just race stuff?

00:10:29.895 --> 00:10:31.864
Because when you talk
about the parents,

00:10:31.864 --> 00:10:34.433
I think the thing that
lit the parents on fire,

00:10:34.433 --> 00:10:37.403 align:start size:97% position:3%
they were pissed, to a
degree, about the race stuff.

00:10:37.403 --> 00:10:39.505
But to me, it was
the gender ideology

00:10:39.505 --> 00:10:42.108 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then the election
of Youngkin in Virginia,

00:10:42.108 --> 00:10:43.876
that that lit that on fire.

00:10:43.876 --> 00:10:47.013 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then we'll talk a bit more
about Disney and DeSantis,

00:10:47.013 --> 00:10:48.981
because you had something
to do with that as well.

00:10:48.981 --> 00:10:52.284
But how the gender piece of
this is connected, oddly,

00:10:52.284 --> 00:10:54.387
to the race piece of this.

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:58.190
- Yeah, I mean, look, if
you look back 50 years

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:00.960 align:start size:84% position:16%
at left-wing ideology,
once they abandoned

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.529
the orthodox Marxist
idea of class conflict.

00:11:04.529 --> 00:11:06.899 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the two great
categories are, you know,

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:08.634
the bourgeoisie and
the proletariat,

00:11:08.634 --> 00:11:12.204
or the capitalist class
and the working class.

00:11:12.204 --> 00:11:15.307
That stopped working by
the mid 20th century.

00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:17.143 align:start size:97% position:3%
And left-wing intellectuals
were looking for

00:11:17.143 --> 00:11:20.211 align:start size:84% position:16%
other soft spots,
other lines of attack,

00:11:20.211 --> 00:11:24.983
and they settled on race
and sex or race and gender.

00:11:24.983 --> 00:11:26.752
And that was the
new formulation.

00:11:26.752 --> 00:11:29.587
And so when we were
dominating the debate

00:11:29.587 --> 00:11:34.292 align:start position:0%
on critical race theory in late
2020, and then really 2021,

00:11:34.292 --> 00:11:36.662 align:start size:88% position:13%
you saw the left retreat
from these issues.

00:11:36.662 --> 00:11:39.865 align:start position:0%
All of a sudden, Robin DiAngelo
was nowhere to be found.

00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:42.735
Ibram Kendi was, you
know, on the defensive.

00:11:42.735 --> 00:11:45.638
And then you saw this
shift in the debate

00:11:45.638 --> 00:11:47.873 align:start size:84% position:16%
when those same
teachers and districts

00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:50.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
that had been pushing
critical race theory in 2021,

00:11:50.843 --> 00:11:53.412
started really doubling
down on, especially,

00:11:53.412 --> 00:11:57.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
trans, non-binary and
queer identity formations

00:11:57.083 --> 00:12:00.619
as the new thing, the new
trend, the new, you know,

00:12:00.619 --> 00:12:04.090 align:start size:88% position:13%
current thing, with
capital C and capital T.

00:12:05.157 --> 00:12:06.624
And they thought that
they could really,

00:12:06.624 --> 00:12:09.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
just shift the debate
and win on these grounds.

00:12:09.061 --> 00:12:12.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so in a sense,
they're complimentary.

00:12:12.364 --> 00:12:15.934
They can play three-card
monte with these two ideas.

00:12:15.934 --> 00:12:18.237
But I think there's
also a deeper nexus

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:20.639
or connection between the two.

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:24.075
You know, students at the
elementary school level,

00:12:24.075 --> 00:12:28.680
high school level, you
know, they can be shamed.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:32.951
They can be told that their
racial category, you know,

00:12:32.951 --> 00:12:34.787
let's say for example,
white students are told

00:12:34.787 --> 00:12:36.288
that they have white privilege,

00:12:36.288 --> 00:12:39.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
white fragility, unconscious
white racial superiority.

00:12:39.792 --> 00:12:43.162 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're part of the
systemically racist society

00:12:43.162 --> 00:12:46.265
by virtue of their ancestry.

00:12:46.265 --> 00:12:49.735
But that doesn't really, in
some ways, help tremendously.

00:12:49.735 --> 00:12:51.070
You can't change your race.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:54.707
They can't do anything
about it at the level

00:12:54.707 --> 00:12:56.475
of their own identity.

00:12:56.475 --> 00:13:01.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
But these sexual
identities offer an escape

00:13:01.113 --> 00:13:03.482 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the guilt and
shame that was instilled

00:13:03.482 --> 00:13:05.751 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the critical race
theory narrative,

00:13:05.751 --> 00:13:07.386
and they offer an escape.

00:13:07.386 --> 00:13:10.189
So you get, in some cases,
you know, I talk to teachers

00:13:10.189 --> 00:13:11.623
that say, "yeah," you know,

00:13:11.623 --> 00:13:14.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
"40% of my white female
students in middle school now,

00:13:14.826 --> 00:13:19.298 align:start size:97% position:3%
identify as non-binary, queer,
transgender, et cetera."

00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:22.434
And so it gives them an
avenue for participation,

00:13:22.434 --> 00:13:24.436 align:start size:81% position:19%
not just at the
level of ideology,

00:13:24.436 --> 00:13:26.372 align:start size:81% position:19%
but the deeper
level of identity.

00:13:26.372 --> 00:13:29.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so in some ways,
critical race theory serves

00:13:29.675 --> 00:13:32.511 align:start size:88% position:13%
to push people into
some of these constructs

00:13:32.511 --> 00:13:33.945
as an avenue out.

00:13:33.945 --> 00:13:37.715 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that we're
seeing that in many places.

00:13:37.715 --> 00:13:41.320
And in some ways, it's actually
been a harder debate to win.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:45.558 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that with CRT we had
a very successful debate.

00:13:45.558 --> 00:13:46.825
And I think that with gender;

00:13:46.825 --> 00:13:48.694
and I'd be curious to
hear your perspective,

00:13:48.694 --> 00:13:50.629
and sexuality, we're still

00:13:50.629 --> 00:13:54.466
in a very kind of
amorphous situation

00:13:54.466 --> 00:13:57.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
where it's not quite clear
which side is winning.

00:13:57.102 --> 00:14:00.105 align:start position:0%
It's not quite queer,
(stammers) quite clear, (laughs)

00:14:00.105 --> 00:14:04.243 align:start size:84% position:16%
(Dave laughs)
both of those apply,

00:14:04.243 --> 00:14:07.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it's not quite queer
or clear, you know,

00:14:07.179 --> 00:14:08.647
where this debate is heading.

00:14:08.647 --> 00:14:10.850 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think there's a
stalemate in some sense,

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.819 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I'm not sure, what's
your interpretation

00:14:13.819 --> 00:14:15.020 align:start size:84% position:16%
of where we are today?
- Yeah, well,

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.354 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, one of the
things that, you know,

00:14:16.354 --> 00:14:18.189 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had Douglas
Murray on last week

00:14:18.189 --> 00:14:20.392 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that we've talked
about for quite some time,

00:14:20.392 --> 00:14:23.929
is that the Ls and
the Gs and the Bs,

00:14:23.929 --> 00:14:27.232
which are a sexuality,
not a sexual identity,

00:14:27.232 --> 00:14:29.735 align:start size:84% position:16%
really need to
separate from the Ts.

00:14:29.735 --> 00:14:33.104
I am not denying, or
gay people, let's say,

00:14:33.104 --> 00:14:35.641
are not denying biology.

00:14:35.641 --> 00:14:38.744
They are not trying to
force a young person

00:14:38.744 --> 00:14:40.813
to alter their body in a way

00:14:40.813 --> 00:14:43.414 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is not congruent
with reality.

00:14:43.414 --> 00:14:45.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, you could have a
personal religious belief

00:14:45.884 --> 00:14:47.786
on homosexuality.

00:14:47.786 --> 00:14:49.021
Personally, I think that's fine,

00:14:49.021 --> 00:14:52.157
as long as you're not
trying to legislate it.

00:14:52.157 --> 00:14:53.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
But there's a
fundamental difference

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:57.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
between sexuality
and sexual identity,

00:14:57.429 --> 00:15:00.532
and that sort of blurring
of reality is the problem.

00:15:00.532 --> 00:15:01.867
So as I often say, and I'm sure

00:15:01.867 --> 00:15:03.134
you've said something similar,

00:15:03.134 --> 00:15:06.605 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's no group
that is more anti-LGB

00:15:06.605 --> 00:15:09.141 align:start size:88% position:13%
than the Ts, and the Ts,
there's also no group

00:15:09.141 --> 00:15:11.743 align:start size:88% position:13%
that is more
anti-female than the Ts,

00:15:11.743 --> 00:15:15.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
who now, want biological
men to beat women in sports.

00:15:15.481 --> 00:15:17.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think the confluence
or the combination

00:15:17.983 --> 00:15:20.853
of those letters has been
really, really dangerous

00:15:20.853 --> 00:15:22.588
for gay people in general.

00:15:23.656 --> 00:15:26.258
- Yeah, that's interesting.

00:15:26.258 --> 00:15:29.294
I do wonder too, I think that
there's something happening

00:15:29.294 --> 00:15:32.130 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the political
right where, you know,

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:35.133
there was a kind of a
concession in a sense,

00:15:35.133 --> 00:15:36.435
on the political right.

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:40.338
The moral majority of
the 1980s and 1990s

00:15:40.338 --> 00:15:42.841
has really disappeared
as a political force.

00:15:42.841 --> 00:15:45.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
You don't see anything
analogous to the Ralph Reeds

00:15:45.644 --> 00:15:49.948
or the Pat Robertsons, you
know, the "700 Club" guys.

00:15:49.948 --> 00:15:52.484
That generation of
social conservative,

00:15:52.484 --> 00:15:55.253
Christian Conservatives has,
I think, really, you know,

00:15:55.253 --> 00:15:56.956
in some cases that
literally died off,

00:15:56.956 --> 00:15:59.558 align:start size:88% position:13%
but in other cases just
kind of disappeared

00:15:59.558 --> 00:16:01.359
as a political force.

00:16:01.359 --> 00:16:05.731 align:start position:0%
There was a period I think,
where there was a kind of truce

00:16:05.731 --> 00:16:07.299
on some of these social issues.

00:16:07.299 --> 00:16:09.501 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so it's been
interesting to watch

00:16:09.501 --> 00:16:11.303
the right start to
change a little bit.

00:16:11.303 --> 00:16:13.905
And I think what's
really led to this,

00:16:13.905 --> 00:16:16.775
and I think, it's
a disastrous move

00:16:16.775 --> 00:16:19.545 align:start size:88% position:13%
on behalf of the radical
gender activists,

00:16:19.545 --> 00:16:21.146
is to actually say now, hey,

00:16:21.146 --> 00:16:24.049 align:start position:0%
we're gonna come into
institutions, come into schools

00:16:24.049 --> 00:16:27.152 align:start size:81% position:19%
and then push, not
just, you know,

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:31.957 align:start size:84% position:16%
tolerance of you know,
of gay sexuality.

00:16:31.957 --> 00:16:34.359 align:start size:88% position:13%
So saying, hey, there
are gay kids in schools.

00:16:34.359 --> 00:16:37.095
They should be, you
know, treated equally
as everyone else.

00:16:37.095 --> 00:16:39.131
They shouldn't be
bullied, shamed,

00:16:39.131 --> 00:16:40.732
or humiliated because
of their sexuality.

00:16:40.732 --> 00:16:43.002
They should be brought
into the wider community.

00:16:43.002 --> 00:16:45.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that virtually
everyone supports

00:16:45.771 --> 00:16:48.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
that kind of basic
dignity and tolerance.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:49.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
But what we're seeing
now, in schools,

00:16:49.741 --> 00:16:51.543 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that they're saying,
they're pushing it

00:16:51.543 --> 00:16:55.047
as an ideology saying, you
know, not just transgender

00:16:55.047 --> 00:16:59.050
or transsexuality, which is,
you know, in its modern sense,

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:01.420
roughly 100 years old,
but they're actually now,

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:05.123
inventing, you know,
genders and sexualities

00:17:05.123 --> 00:17:07.859 align:start size:88% position:13%
of, you know,
non-binary, two-spirit,

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:11.429 align:start position:0%
all of these boutique
identities that in a real sense,

00:17:11.429 --> 00:17:13.198
are fake categories.

00:17:14.232 --> 00:17:17.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they're pushing
them onto kids

00:17:17.469 --> 00:17:22.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're really desiring
and really using kids,

00:17:23.208 --> 00:17:25.343
that are impressionable,

00:17:25.343 --> 00:17:27.746 align:start size:84% position:16%
as pawns in a greater
political game.

00:17:27.746 --> 00:17:30.382
And I think that's what people
are really revolting against.

00:17:30.382 --> 00:17:31.883 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're saying,
hey, wait a minute,

00:17:31.883 --> 00:17:34.686 align:start size:97% position:3%
you have a policy where
you can secretly transition

00:17:34.686 --> 00:17:38.557 align:start size:88% position:13%
my child into a
non-binary, they, them,

00:17:38.557 --> 00:17:42.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then push the
identity into a political

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:45.563 align:start size:88% position:13%
activist posture without
even notifying me,

00:17:45.563 --> 00:17:48.133
as the parent, is so
deeply manipulative.

00:17:48.133 --> 00:17:51.536
And I think that, you
know, it is, I hope,

00:17:51.536 --> 00:17:53.838 align:start size:94% position:6%
a miscalculation on
behalf of these activists

00:17:53.838 --> 00:17:56.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I just don't
think that that can stand

00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:57.476
over the long term.

00:17:57.476 --> 00:17:58.977
A lot of people on the
right are uncomfortable

00:17:58.977 --> 00:18:00.278
talking about these issues.

00:18:00.278 --> 00:18:03.648
Uncomfortable, you know,
activating against them.

00:18:03.648 --> 00:18:06.818
But as we've seen with
all the detransitioners,

00:18:06.818 --> 00:18:09.922
we see with these
horror stories,

00:18:11.023 --> 00:18:13.191
it's untenable in
the medium term.

00:18:13.191 --> 00:18:15.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so I think, that
while the debate might be

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:17.496
a bit in kind of
a stalemate now,

00:18:17.496 --> 00:18:19.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think, that ultimately,
it's got to shift,

00:18:19.864 --> 00:18:21.532 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's got to go
back to the center.

00:18:21.532 --> 00:18:24.102 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, well, you're right
about what a damn shame it is,

00:18:24.102 --> 00:18:27.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
because for, you're
right, the moral majority,

00:18:27.405 --> 00:18:29.908
the Rick Santorums,
Mike Huckabee types,

00:18:29.908 --> 00:18:31.710
they've moved on
from these things.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:34.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
Trump ran, first term
around, you know,

00:18:34.412 --> 00:18:37.949 align:start position:0%
first term, as pro-gay
marriage, people put it to bed.

00:18:37.949 --> 00:18:40.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then they've just
rehashed all of this stuff.

00:18:40.552 --> 00:18:43.222 align:start size:81% position:19%
And the average
gay person really,

00:18:43.222 --> 00:18:44.323
wants nothing to do with it.

00:18:44.323 --> 00:18:45.991
But you know, the activist class

00:18:45.991 --> 00:18:47.192
always needs something to do.

00:18:47.192 --> 00:18:48.493
But to that point, you know,

00:18:48.493 --> 00:18:51.229
as we've exited Gay Pride month,

00:18:51.229 --> 00:18:54.333
and thankfully, we can go
back to shame, hallelujah.

00:18:54.333 --> 00:18:55.800
You know, there were so many,

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:58.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
that ridiculous flag that
once was the rainbow,

00:18:58.503 --> 00:18:59.671
that included everything.

00:18:59.671 --> 00:19:01.840 align:start size:88% position:13%
But now they've
politicized the rainbow

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:04.075
with this triangle and
the circle and the brown

00:19:04.075 --> 00:19:05.577
and the whole thing.

00:19:05.577 --> 00:19:07.278
The fact that they're putting
that up at the White House,

00:19:07.278 --> 00:19:09.047
when you see that up
at the White House,

00:19:09.047 --> 00:19:10.515
equal with the American flag,

00:19:10.515 --> 00:19:12.718 align:start size:97% position:3%
which actually might be a
violation of the Constitution,

00:19:12.718 --> 00:19:14.152
or when you see that hanging

00:19:14.152 --> 00:19:16.954
outside of the Canadian
Prime Minister's office,

00:19:16.954 --> 00:19:20.625 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think most people don't
realize it's a political flag.

00:19:20.625 --> 00:19:23.195 align:start size:97% position:3%
People think, oh, it's
just celebrating sexuality,

00:19:23.195 --> 00:19:25.731 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's actually
political in nature.

00:19:25.731 --> 00:19:27.865 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's really what
you're saying.

00:19:27.865 --> 00:19:31.569
- Yeah, 100%, I mean, it
is a political movement,

00:19:31.569 --> 00:19:34.639 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it's a political movement
with its own iconography,

00:19:34.639 --> 00:19:37.910 align:start size:97% position:3%
with its own, you know, kind
of propaganda messaging.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:41.513 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's like any other
political movement,

00:19:41.513 --> 00:19:43.114
it has its set of symbols.

00:19:43.114 --> 00:19:46.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
And certainly, what I
think of, when Biden hung

00:19:46.718 --> 00:19:49.821
the Progress Pride Flag,
I guess it's called now,

00:19:49.821 --> 00:19:51.490 align:start size:84% position:16%
the one with all the
different shapes.

00:19:51.490 --> 00:19:53.158
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:19:53.158 --> 00:19:56.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Look, it's a basic
political patronage move.

00:19:56.461 --> 00:20:00.431 align:start size:97% position:3%
Biden knows where the energy
is on the activist part

00:20:00.431 --> 00:20:02.333
of his political base.

00:20:02.333 --> 00:20:05.336
He knows that this is
a very important part

00:20:05.336 --> 00:20:06.571
of his coalition.

00:20:06.571 --> 00:20:09.073
And he is essentially,
rewarding the activists

00:20:09.073 --> 00:20:12.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
within that coalition
with this symbolic display

00:20:12.877 --> 00:20:15.413
and kind of White
House ceremony.

00:20:15.413 --> 00:20:19.250
You know, I think
that in a sense,

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:21.987
it's kind of normal
patronage politics.

00:20:21.987 --> 00:20:24.489
And so that's how I view that.

00:20:24.489 --> 00:20:27.358
And Biden is, you know,
for all of his faults

00:20:27.358 --> 00:20:30.762
and for all of his
seeming, you know,

00:20:30.762 --> 00:20:35.434 align:start size:97% position:3%
kind of slow kind of mental
capacities as he ages,

00:20:35.434 --> 00:20:37.836
he knows which way
the wind is blowing.

00:20:37.836 --> 00:20:40.439 align:start size:88% position:13%
He knows where the
Democratic Coalition is.

00:20:40.439 --> 00:20:42.674 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think that it
shows really, the power

00:20:42.674 --> 00:20:44.042
of that activist narrative,

00:20:44.042 --> 00:20:46.578
the power of that
activist apparatus,

00:20:46.578 --> 00:20:49.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they get their
flag hung up, you know,

00:20:51.149 --> 00:20:53.785
where really, only kind
of the American flag

00:20:53.785 --> 00:20:56.188
or the national flag should
be hanging, in my opinion.

00:20:56.188 --> 00:20:58.890
And I think that the
debate about flags

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:00.926
and symbols is really important.

00:21:01.993 --> 00:21:03.795
The things that we
elevate symbolically,

00:21:03.795 --> 00:21:05.931
are the things that we value

00:21:05.931 --> 00:21:07.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
in a more human
and meaningful way.

00:21:07.732 --> 00:21:10.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so we should be quite
careful and quite deliberate

00:21:10.735 --> 00:21:14.039 align:start position:0%
and have really, an open debate
about what kind of symbols

00:21:14.039 --> 00:21:15.941
that we put in places
of national prominence.

00:21:15.941 --> 00:21:18.276 align:start size:88% position:13%
And certainly, in a
public school, you know,

00:21:18.276 --> 00:21:20.545
you couldn't hang a crucifix

00:21:20.545 --> 00:21:23.348
at the top of the public
school, a religious symbol.

00:21:23.348 --> 00:21:26.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, you couldn't
hang a, you know,

00:21:26.685 --> 00:21:29.154 align:start size:81% position:19%
partisan political
flag at the top

00:21:29.154 --> 00:21:30.855
of a public school classroom.

00:21:30.855 --> 00:21:33.224
And so I think that
it's very interesting

00:21:33.224 --> 00:21:34.926
that the Pride and
Progress Pride Flag

00:21:34.926 --> 00:21:38.396 align:start size:84% position:16%
are given an exemption
for restrictions

00:21:38.396 --> 00:21:41.667
on religious and political
symbolism in classrooms.

00:21:41.667 --> 00:21:46.638
It's a pretty sophisticated
reason that that is the case.

00:21:47.873 --> 00:21:52.244
They've been very good
at kind of laundering

00:21:52.244 --> 00:21:57.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
the political message that
is the essence of this,

00:21:58.717 --> 00:22:01.987
with kind of vague, tolerant,
diversity, equity, inclusion,

00:22:01.987 --> 00:22:05.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
surface level messaging that
allows it to be displayed

00:22:05.223 --> 00:22:10.295 align:start size:88% position:13%
in places where the
kind of analogous flags

00:22:11.429 --> 00:22:12.530
of any other political
or religious movement

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:13.698
would not be allowed,

00:22:13.698 --> 00:22:15.166 align:start size:84% position:16%
would not even really
be contemplated.

00:22:15.166 --> 00:22:17.335 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, well, they say
it's all about inclusivity,

00:22:17.335 --> 00:22:19.704 align:start size:81% position:19%
but that flag does
not include me.

00:22:19.704 --> 00:22:22.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
It doesn't include Douglas
Murray or Peter Thiel

00:22:22.139 --> 00:22:25.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
or Spencer Klavan, or
the litany, Rick Grenell,

00:22:25.143 --> 00:22:26.812
or plenty of other gay people.

00:22:26.812 --> 00:22:28.213
But let's move on for a sec,

00:22:28.213 --> 00:22:30.548 align:start position:0%
because one of the other
things that sort of caught fire

00:22:30.548 --> 00:22:34.552
in the last year, was the
war that DeSantis went at

00:22:34.552 --> 00:22:37.556
with Disney, and you
were a piece of that

00:22:37.556 --> 00:22:38.923
at a couple different fronts.

00:22:38.923 --> 00:22:41.125 align:start size:88% position:13%
But am I correct, you
were the one that leaked

00:22:41.125 --> 00:22:43.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
the original Disney
videos, right?

00:22:43.795 --> 00:22:44.929
- Yeah, that's right.

00:22:44.929 --> 00:22:47.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah, I have a
source inside Disney,

00:22:47.032 --> 00:22:49.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
actually, multiple
sources inside Disney.

00:22:49.401 --> 00:22:51.536
I had reported on their
critical race theory

00:22:51.536 --> 00:22:54.139
employee training
program in 2020,

00:22:54.139 --> 00:22:56.208
and then, or in 2021 rather.

00:22:56.208 --> 00:23:00.112 align:start position:0%
And in 2022, as the Disney
fight was brewing with DeSantis,

00:23:00.112 --> 00:23:01.846 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of my sources
leaked me these really,

00:23:01.846 --> 00:23:04.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
incredible videos from
inside the company,

00:23:04.882 --> 00:23:07.318
as well as some other
supporting documents,

00:23:07.318 --> 00:23:08.887
that kicked it off.

00:23:08.887 --> 00:23:11.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
And even Governor DeSantis,
at the bill signing,

00:23:11.223 --> 00:23:13.625 align:start size:88% position:13%
stripping Disney of
its special privileges,

00:23:13.625 --> 00:23:15.460 align:start size:97% position:3%
he said during the ceremony,
he said, "You know,

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:17.162 align:start size:84% position:16%
if Rufo hadn't
leaked these videos,

00:23:17.162 --> 00:23:18.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know if
we'd even be here."

00:23:18.629 --> 00:23:20.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was really, a pivotal
moment in this fight.

00:23:20.832 --> 00:23:24.236
And it's an important fight
because it not only marks

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:27.772
a fight about gender
ideology in schools;

00:23:27.772 --> 00:23:29.441
you'll remember that that's
what this was all about,

00:23:29.441 --> 00:23:31.609
the Parental Rights
and Education Act.

00:23:31.609 --> 00:23:34.079
But it also marks a shift
on the political right

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:39.583
against this libertarian
corporatist, you know,

00:23:39.583 --> 00:23:42.554 align:start position:0%
we can call it a neoliberal
tendency on the political right,

00:23:42.554 --> 00:23:44.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the corporations
are always right.

00:23:44.423 --> 00:23:46.190 align:start size:81% position:19%
We never fight
with corporations.

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:49.361
We never get crosswise
with the Fortune 100.

00:23:49.361 --> 00:23:51.629
DeSantis changed that
very dramatically.

00:23:51.629 --> 00:23:53.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
And although I
think it, you know,

00:23:53.131 --> 00:23:55.700
it kind of enraged libertarians,

00:23:57.169 --> 00:24:00.471
I think most Conservatives are
now thinking, wait a minute,

00:24:00.471 --> 00:24:03.775
these companies are not just
creating products and services,

00:24:03.775 --> 00:24:06.210
you know, adding to the GDP,

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:09.580 align:start size:94% position:6%
but they're actually
waging ideological fights.

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:13.751 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're wading into
partisan cultural fights.

00:24:13.751 --> 00:24:16.688
And they're now, crosswise
with Conservative voters.

00:24:16.688 --> 00:24:18.823
And we need to have
political leaders

00:24:18.823 --> 00:24:20.925
that are willing to stand up,

00:24:20.925 --> 00:24:25.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
not just to our traditional
opponents on the left,

00:24:25.430 --> 00:24:29.367
but even these new opponents

00:24:29.367 --> 00:24:31.369
within our traditional coalition

00:24:31.369 --> 00:24:34.606
that have now, deviated from
support of the political right

00:24:34.606 --> 00:24:36.608
and waded into the culture war

00:24:36.608 --> 00:24:39.311
on behalf of the cultural left.

00:24:40.212 --> 00:24:42.180
- Right, so when that happened,

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:46.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then DeSantis decides
to use state power

00:24:46.384 --> 00:24:48.786
to push back against
it, were you surprised

00:24:48.786 --> 00:24:51.489 align:start size:97% position:3%
to see some of the, you know,
whether we wanna call 'em

00:24:51.489 --> 00:24:54.192 align:start size:84% position:16%
Neoliberals or
fake Conservatives,

00:24:54.192 --> 00:24:55.960
you know, guys like Mike Pence,

00:24:55.960 --> 00:24:57.896
oh this is too much
government power.

00:24:57.896 --> 00:25:00.531 align:start size:84% position:16%
Nikki Haley, too much
government power,

00:25:00.531 --> 00:25:03.467
when finally, a Conservative
was actually doing something

00:25:03.467 --> 00:25:06.771 align:start size:97% position:3%
about the things that they're
always complaining about?

00:25:06.771 --> 00:25:08.339
- Yeah, I mean, I
wasn't surprised,

00:25:08.339 --> 00:25:10.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I was certainly,
frustrated and disappointed

00:25:10.975 --> 00:25:14.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they're just repeating
this brain dead script

00:25:14.845 --> 00:25:17.782
that has been programmed
in them since the 1980s.

00:25:17.782 --> 00:25:20.317
But it's not the 1980s anymore.

00:25:20.317 --> 00:25:23.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
And while that was
probably the right ideology

00:25:23.021 --> 00:25:25.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
or philosophy or
policy for the time,

00:25:25.423 --> 00:25:26.691
things have shifted a bit.

00:25:26.691 --> 00:25:28.426
And even on their
own terms, though,

00:25:28.426 --> 00:25:30.729 align:start size:97% position:3%
I argue that with libertarians
all the time on this,

00:25:30.729 --> 00:25:32.196
and then sometimes they concede.

00:25:32.196 --> 00:25:35.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
I say, "wait a minute, even
on libertarian grounds,

00:25:35.033 --> 00:25:36.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think DeSantis did
the right thing."

00:25:36.534 --> 00:25:38.069
And they say, "What?

00:25:38.069 --> 00:25:39.904
He interfered with a private
company, what do you mean?"

00:25:39.904 --> 00:25:41.406
I say, "Well, look at it."

00:25:41.406 --> 00:25:45.977
Disney, in decades before
DeSantis was in office,

00:25:45.977 --> 00:25:49.146
used their economic power,
used their lobbying power,

00:25:49.146 --> 00:25:53.318
and used their cultural power
to extract special privileges

00:25:53.318 --> 00:25:54.953
from the state of Florida.

00:25:54.953 --> 00:25:56.487
They had special tax structure,

00:25:56.487 --> 00:26:00.224
special regulatory structure,
special real estate structure.

00:26:00.224 --> 00:26:01.993
You know, they had
the right to create

00:26:01.993 --> 00:26:04.996
their own nuclear power
plant at some point,

00:26:04.996 --> 00:26:06.965
through the kind
of sweetheart deals

00:26:06.965 --> 00:26:08.466
they'd negotiated
with the state.

00:26:08.466 --> 00:26:10.268 align:start size:84% position:16%
And these are
special privileges,

00:26:10.268 --> 00:26:12.237 align:start size:97% position:3%
crony capitalist privileges-
- Right.

00:26:12.237 --> 00:26:13.804
- That none of their competitors

00:26:13.804 --> 00:26:15.406
and none of the other
private companies had.

00:26:15.406 --> 00:26:16.607 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in fact, DeSantis didn't-
- SeaWorld,

00:26:16.607 --> 00:26:18.043
does it have a nuke?

00:26:19.344 --> 00:26:21.312 align:start position:0%
- SeaWorld does not have nukes,
although maybe it should,

00:26:21.312 --> 00:26:22.480 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, and send them
(Dave laughs)

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.515 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the Ukraine or
something, I don't know.

00:26:24.515 --> 00:26:27.986
But you know, none of
the other companies

00:26:27.986 --> 00:26:29.487
had these privilege, so in fact,

00:26:29.487 --> 00:26:32.924
DeSantis was doing nothing
but level the playing field

00:26:32.924 --> 00:26:35.560 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that Disney had to
compete with all the others.

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:38.229 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it created actually,
a more free market

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:41.432
and a less crony
capitalist system.

00:26:41.432 --> 00:26:43.201
And of course, then
they turn very silent

00:26:43.201 --> 00:26:44.969
and they say, uh oh,

00:26:44.969 --> 00:26:47.639
I can't fall back on
my ideology anymore.

00:26:47.639 --> 00:26:50.241 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, Ayn Rand is
somewhere in the corner,

00:26:50.241 --> 00:26:52.009 align:start size:81% position:19%
you know, crying.
(Dave laughs)

00:26:52.009 --> 00:26:53.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, we
actually have to have

00:26:53.344 --> 00:26:54.779 align:start size:84% position:16%
a substantive
debate about culture

00:26:54.779 --> 00:26:56.180
and Libertarians
are very nervous.

00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:59.050
They want to be accepted
by the left on culture.

00:27:01.018 --> 00:27:04.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
But what I think it really
shows, is that on all grounds,

00:27:04.889 --> 00:27:07.892
except for the simple
friend-enemy grounds,

00:27:07.892 --> 00:27:10.161 align:start size:81% position:19%
if you're, you
know, on the left,

00:27:10.161 --> 00:27:13.298
I think that his move was
defensible on all grounds.

00:27:13.298 --> 00:27:15.032 align:start size:88% position:13%
And not just defensible
on all grounds,

00:27:15.032 --> 00:27:19.303 align:start size:97% position:3%
but really, put the priorities
in the correct rank order.

00:27:19.303 --> 00:27:23.941
It said, look, protecting the
flourishing of our society

00:27:23.941 --> 00:27:27.045
and of our kids and of our
families is more important

00:27:27.045 --> 00:27:29.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
than protecting
corporate interests.

00:27:29.214 --> 00:27:30.748
To me, that is the fundamental.

00:27:30.748 --> 00:27:33.217
America is about the
protection of life,

00:27:33.217 --> 00:27:35.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
liberty and the
pursuit of happiness.

00:27:35.787 --> 00:27:38.556
And our economy
and our companies

00:27:38.556 --> 00:27:41.926 align:start size:84% position:16%
should all be
thought of as means,

00:27:41.926 --> 00:27:44.929
in order to achieve the
end, of human happiness.

00:27:44.929 --> 00:27:46.997
So I think DeSantis
is in the right,

00:27:46.997 --> 00:27:49.600
both, looking at our
founding principles,

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:51.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
looking at libertarian
principles,

00:27:51.068 --> 00:27:52.837
and looking at what
might be considered

00:27:52.837 --> 00:27:54.772
social conservative principles.

00:27:54.772 --> 00:27:57.074
- So as long as we're
talking about DeSantis

00:27:57.074 --> 00:27:58.809 align:start size:84% position:16%
doing some stuff
right-related to this,

00:27:58.809 --> 00:28:00.779
I think one of the
other institutions

00:28:00.779 --> 00:28:02.881
that's done some
stuff right lately,

00:28:02.881 --> 00:28:04.915
obviously, is the Supreme Court.

00:28:04.915 --> 00:28:07.418
I take it, you are
pretty happy about

00:28:07.418 --> 00:28:10.821 align:start position:0%
the reversal of this affirmative
action decision at Harvard.

00:28:10.821 --> 00:28:12.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if you wanna
talk about, you know,

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:14.058
what you're doing
here in Florida,

00:28:14.058 --> 00:28:15.526
that would be fine as well.

00:28:15.526 --> 00:28:18.796 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that basically, we
are removing some of this,

00:28:18.796 --> 00:28:22.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
what some might call,
structural or systemic racism

00:28:22.667 --> 00:28:23.568
from the system.

00:28:24.402 --> 00:28:25.336
- Yeah, that's right.

00:28:25.336 --> 00:28:26.771
I think this is a huge ruling,

00:28:26.771 --> 00:28:28.206 align:start size:84% position:16%
although it's
certainly not enough.

00:28:28.206 --> 00:28:29.874 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'd like to see some
other precedents

00:28:29.874 --> 00:28:34.946
such as Griggs versus Duke
Power Company overturned.

00:28:36.213 --> 00:28:37.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, we have to get to-
- What is that one?

00:28:37.481 --> 00:28:39.918
- Sure, it's what
really established

00:28:39.918 --> 00:28:41.486
the notion of disparate impact.

00:28:41.486 --> 00:28:46.558
So if a policy or a
system is neutral,

00:28:47.625 --> 00:28:49.060 align:start size:88% position:13%
it doesn't favor one
racial group or another,

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:51.229 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it creates
disparate outcomes.

00:28:51.229 --> 00:28:54.598
You can read racism into
that, that could be deemed

00:28:54.598 --> 00:28:56.100
illegal discrimination.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:29:00.572
And so to say, for example,
that elderly Asian females

00:29:01.906 --> 00:29:05.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
are incarcerated at a
lower rate than, you know,

00:29:06.845 --> 00:29:08.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
young white males
or young Black males

00:29:08.813 --> 00:29:10.849
or young Latino males, you know,

00:29:10.849 --> 00:29:12.283
you could read into that saying,

00:29:12.283 --> 00:29:14.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
oh, that's a disparate
outcome and therefore racist.

00:29:14.419 --> 00:29:16.053
We have to interrogate it.

00:29:16.053 --> 00:29:18.155
I think we have to get
to, on the other hand,

00:29:18.155 --> 00:29:23.094
a system of colorblind
equality, very simple.

00:29:23.094 --> 00:29:25.764
We treat you as an individual,

00:29:27.398 --> 00:29:30.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
equally under the
law with due process.

00:29:30.434 --> 00:29:33.704
We don't discriminate
against you on cause

00:29:33.704 --> 00:29:37.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
of your race or ancestry,
but you're actually

00:29:37.575 --> 00:29:39.711
put into our system
and you'll be judged

00:29:39.711 --> 00:29:41.145
on your individual merit.

00:29:41.145 --> 00:29:44.148 align:start size:97% position:3%
That is the only kind of
system that can be successful

00:29:44.148 --> 00:29:46.050
at the kind of scale that
we have with, you know,

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:48.586
more than 300 million
people in our country.

00:29:48.586 --> 00:29:53.024 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can't treat
people as reflections

00:29:53.024 --> 00:29:55.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
of a racial group or as
symbols of a racial group.

00:29:55.760 --> 00:29:56.995
You have to treat
people equally.

00:29:56.995 --> 00:29:59.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I think,
we're getting there

00:29:59.864 --> 00:30:01.466
and we're getting there slowly.

00:30:01.466 --> 00:30:05.336
The Supreme Court is quite
a big help on this path,

00:30:05.336 --> 00:30:07.004 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we have to
really, push further.

00:30:07.004 --> 00:30:10.475
I want everyone to feel that
they're given a fair shot,

00:30:10.475 --> 00:30:12.143
that they're treated equally,

00:30:12.143 --> 00:30:15.446
and that their outcomes
in life are of course,

00:30:15.446 --> 00:30:18.049
dependent on where they
came from, their origins,

00:30:18.049 --> 00:30:21.052
but also dependent on their
hard work and fair treatment.

00:30:21.052 --> 00:30:22.954
So that what people earn,

00:30:22.954 --> 00:30:25.156 align:start size:88% position:13%
they really feel like
that they have earned it

00:30:25.156 --> 00:30:26.657
through their own efforts.

00:30:26.657 --> 00:30:29.193
They haven't been punished
or rewarded on anything else

00:30:29.193 --> 00:30:32.897
besides their own efforts,
their own contributions.

00:30:32.897 --> 00:30:35.166
- What do you do, you
think, with the people

00:30:35.166 --> 00:30:38.603
that use every positive thing

00:30:38.603 --> 00:30:40.405
that comes out of the system

00:30:40.405 --> 00:30:42.507
as proof of the
system's failure?

00:30:42.507 --> 00:30:44.576
In other words, so we
get a good decision,

00:30:44.576 --> 00:30:46.311
let's say that you
and I are agreeing,

00:30:46.311 --> 00:30:48.446
from the Supreme Court
on affirmative action.

00:30:48.446 --> 00:30:51.883 align:start size:88% position:13%
But then if you watch
MSNBC, or you watch CNN,

00:30:51.883 --> 00:30:54.552
you listen to AOC
or Elizabeth Warren

00:30:54.552 --> 00:30:58.156
or Kamala Harris or Joe
Biden, for all of them

00:30:58.156 --> 00:31:00.825
it was proof that the
court is corrupted.

00:31:00.825 --> 00:31:03.360
Thus proof, we could
probably take this back

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
to critical race theory,
proof that the system itself

00:31:06.297 --> 00:31:08.065
is designed against the people.

00:31:08.065 --> 00:31:10.401
That seems like an
intractable problem,

00:31:10.401 --> 00:31:13.137
that every time we try
to free the people more,

00:31:13.137 --> 00:31:15.773
that the certain
set of activists,

00:31:15.773 --> 00:31:17.609
which now includes
people literally,

00:31:17.609 --> 00:31:19.177
like the president
of the United States,

00:31:19.177 --> 00:31:22.447 align:start size:97% position:3%
use that decision to
undermine the system itself.

00:31:23.548 --> 00:31:25.416
- Yeah, I mean, I think
it's a dangerous game

00:31:25.416 --> 00:31:26.618
that they're playing.

00:31:26.618 --> 00:31:31.488 align:start size:88% position:13%
They interpret every
political loss for them,

00:31:31.488 --> 00:31:35.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
as evidence of
corruption, racism,

00:31:35.893 --> 00:31:39.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
then that justifies the
need to abolish the system,

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:42.867
reform the system,
pack the courts.

00:31:42.867 --> 00:31:47.038
And so it's a circularly
self-reinforcing argument.

00:31:47.038 --> 00:31:48.306
Every time they win,

00:31:48.306 --> 00:31:50.842
it's evidence of the
virtue of their ideas.

00:31:50.842 --> 00:31:52.277
Every time they lose

00:31:52.277 --> 00:31:54.846 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's evidence of the
corruption of their enemies.

00:31:54.846 --> 00:31:58.316
And to a certain extent,
all politics is like this.

00:31:58.316 --> 00:32:01.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, certainly, many
people on the political right,

00:32:01.118 --> 00:32:03.254
and many people on the
political left share

00:32:03.254 --> 00:32:04.689
in that mode of thinking.

00:32:04.689 --> 00:32:07.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's a basic partisan,
reflexive thinking.

00:32:08.293 --> 00:32:13.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
However, we really
have to, in this case,

00:32:14.665 --> 00:32:15.600 align:start position:0%
on the political right, we
really have to shift the debate

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:17.135
to the substance and the merit.

00:32:17.135 --> 00:32:18.803
One thing too, that
was totally lost

00:32:18.803 --> 00:32:20.571 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the Supreme Court
decision, is that, you know,

00:32:20.571 --> 00:32:23.640
they were saying, oh, this is
evidence of white supremacy.

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:26.411
But the plaintiffs in
the Supreme Court case

00:32:26.411 --> 00:32:28.379
were Asian Americans, you know?

00:32:28.379 --> 00:32:29.647 align:start size:97% position:3%
(Dave laughs)
That was like, totally lost

00:32:29.647 --> 00:32:30.814 align:start size:88% position:13%
in all of the reporting.
- Asians are the new

00:32:30.814 --> 00:32:32.316 align:start size:84% position:16%
white supremacists,
dude, come on.

00:32:32.316 --> 00:32:33.484
- Yeah, exactly.

00:32:33.484 --> 00:32:35.486
The Asian white supremacists,

00:32:35.486 --> 00:32:36.854
the Chinese immigrant
white supremacists.

00:32:36.854 --> 00:32:39.790
I mean, you know, so
we have to really,

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:43.193 align:start size:97% position:3%
what I found most effective
in all of my debates

00:32:43.193 --> 00:32:46.997
and narrative, kind of
warfare with the left

00:32:46.997 --> 00:32:49.367
is that the more
you can stick to

00:32:49.367 --> 00:32:54.339
the very specific, concrete,
tangible elements of the story,

00:32:55.573 --> 00:32:58.009
the more that you can
make a persuasive case

00:32:58.009 --> 00:33:02.446
that is substantive, that
sticks to reported matter

00:33:02.446 --> 00:33:05.049
or sticks to the real
concrete narrative,

00:33:05.049 --> 00:33:08.051 align:start size:84% position:16%
the more likelihood
we have to win.

00:33:08.051 --> 00:33:09.486 align:start size:84% position:16%
We did it on
critical race theory.

00:33:09.486 --> 00:33:12.824 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think we're doing it
slowly, you know, on gender.

00:33:12.824 --> 00:33:14.591 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think we can
also do it on policies

00:33:14.591 --> 00:33:17.195 align:start size:88% position:13%
like affirmative action,
where, by the way,

00:33:17.195 --> 00:33:19.130
Americans of all
racial backgrounds,

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:23.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
white, Black, Latino,
Asian, all those,

00:33:23.167 --> 00:33:26.503
the major racial groups,
oppose affirmative action.

00:33:26.503 --> 00:33:30.107
And so we have to take
that public sentiment,

00:33:30.107 --> 00:33:33.944 align:start size:94% position:6%
we have to reinforce it
with a political narrative

00:33:33.944 --> 00:33:37.582
and substantive reporting
and court cases, et cetera,

00:33:37.582 --> 00:33:39.016
and then start to actually say,

00:33:39.016 --> 00:33:41.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know what, we're
gonna take this momentum.

00:33:41.085 --> 00:33:44.388
We're gonna take all of this
goodwill and public support

00:33:44.388 --> 00:33:46.223
and start to change the system.

00:33:46.223 --> 00:33:48.626
And look, Republicans
have been very wary

00:33:48.626 --> 00:33:52.162 align:start size:88% position:13%
of any reforms to our
civil rights bureaucracy

00:33:52.162 --> 00:33:53.664
in the United States.

00:33:53.664 --> 00:33:56.467 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's time that we start
pushing in that direction.

00:33:56.467 --> 00:34:00.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have to reform the
Civil Rights Act of 1991,

00:34:00.404 --> 00:34:05.108 align:start position:0%
which codifies disparate impact
analysis into federal law.

00:34:05.108 --> 00:34:09.280
We have to move beyond that and
reform our civil rights law,

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:12.250 align:start position:0%
and in some ways, really
strengthen our civil rights law

00:34:12.250 --> 00:34:15.953 align:start size:88% position:13%
in order to ensure full
colorblind equality

00:34:15.953 --> 00:34:17.422
for everyone in our country.

00:34:18.756 --> 00:34:23.627
- What do you think about the
idea that some states now,

00:34:23.627 --> 00:34:25.263 align:start size:81% position:19%
will just continue
to do it right,

00:34:25.263 --> 00:34:27.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
and some states will
continue to do it wrong?

00:34:27.331 --> 00:34:30.234 align:start position:0%
So as you know, I very
publicly left Cali for Florida.

00:34:30.234 --> 00:34:32.036
I feel like I live in a
different country here.

00:34:32.036 --> 00:34:34.305
I mean, the set of
values in this state

00:34:34.305 --> 00:34:37.442 align:start size:84% position:16%
are 180 different than
they were in Cali.

00:34:37.442 --> 00:34:40.578
Cali is continuing
to go down that road.

00:34:40.578 --> 00:34:42.279
Florida is stopping that.

00:34:42.279 --> 00:34:44.682 align:start size:97% position:3%
Tennessee, let's say Texas,
more in line with us.

00:34:44.682 --> 00:34:48.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
New York, Connecticut,
Jersey, going down that road.

00:34:48.051 --> 00:34:51.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you sense that it will
just continue to go that way?

00:34:51.622 --> 00:34:54.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
And also that's partly because
of the migration of people,

00:34:54.025 --> 00:34:55.659 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the blues
are gonna get bluer

00:34:55.659 --> 00:34:57.461
and the reds are
gonna get redder,

00:34:57.461 --> 00:34:59.797
and does it even
matter, I guess?

00:34:59.797 --> 00:35:02.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, I mean, it appears
that that is the trend,

00:35:02.333 --> 00:35:04.102 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I anticipate that
the trend will continue.

00:35:04.102 --> 00:35:06.504 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I actually think
that, in a larger sense,

00:35:06.504 --> 00:35:08.873
a structural sense,
that that's good.

00:35:08.873 --> 00:35:11.308 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's exactly how our
government was intended to be.

00:35:11.308 --> 00:35:13.510 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were intended to
be a federalist system

00:35:13.510 --> 00:35:16.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
with states that can pursue
very different visions

00:35:16.747 --> 00:35:19.349 align:start size:88% position:13%
of governance, very
different sets of values

00:35:19.349 --> 00:35:21.652
that they orient
as their kind of

00:35:21.652 --> 00:35:23.887
hierarchy of values, let's say.

00:35:23.887 --> 00:35:27.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, in a sense, I like
to see that California

00:35:27.424 --> 00:35:30.527 align:start size:81% position:19%
and Florida and
Idaho and New York

00:35:30.527 --> 00:35:33.030 align:start size:97% position:3%
are pursuing very different
visions of government.

00:35:33.030 --> 00:35:36.033
It gives the citizens of
every state the ability

00:35:36.033 --> 00:35:39.203 align:start size:97% position:3%
to look at what's happening,
to judge and compare,

00:35:39.203 --> 00:35:40.737
to decide what they want.

00:35:40.737 --> 00:35:43.140
And they can either fight and
advocate within their states,

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:45.709
or they can move to another
state that's more friendly.

00:35:45.709 --> 00:35:47.211
This is actually good.

00:35:47.211 --> 00:35:50.881
But there's one big caveat,
states such as California,

00:35:50.881 --> 00:35:53.717
should not be allowed
to violate federal law,

00:35:53.717 --> 00:35:55.152
and should not be
allowed to violate

00:35:55.152 --> 00:35:56.954
the principles of
our constitution.

00:35:56.954 --> 00:35:59.156
And so when states
like California,

00:35:59.156 --> 00:36:02.359
I recently exposed a
UC Berkeley Law dean,

00:36:02.359 --> 00:36:04.961 align:start size:97% position:3%
saying that even though
affirmative action is illegal

00:36:04.961 --> 00:36:07.398 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the state of California,
we do it secretly,

00:36:07.398 --> 00:36:08.699
and this is how we do it.

00:36:08.699 --> 00:36:10.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yep.
- That's not just a policy

00:36:10.768 --> 00:36:12.903
that California can
have or not have.

00:36:12.903 --> 00:36:14.872
That's a violation of
the 14th Amendment.

00:36:14.872 --> 00:36:17.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as the law
professor, you know,

00:36:17.675 --> 00:36:20.377
Dean Chemerinsky
should know this.

00:36:20.377 --> 00:36:23.180
But we should be cracking
down at the federal level

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:25.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
for any kind of violation
of the constitution,

00:36:25.749 --> 00:36:29.019 align:start size:84% position:16%
while giving states
the latitude to pursue

00:36:29.019 --> 00:36:31.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
their own vision in their
other state policies.

00:36:31.888 --> 00:36:34.324
And so, look, I live
in Washington state,

00:36:34.324 --> 00:36:37.227 align:start size:88% position:13%
which has its advantages
and disadvantages.

00:36:37.227 --> 00:36:40.430
I spend a lot of time in your
neck of the woods, in Florida.

00:36:40.430 --> 00:36:42.266
I see the difference.

00:36:42.266 --> 00:36:45.535
I'm holding out strong
here, but I certainly,

00:36:45.535 --> 00:36:48.104
appreciate the fact that
states like Florida, Texas,

00:36:48.104 --> 00:36:53.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
Idaho, Tennessee, are
pursuing a different path

00:36:54.278 --> 00:36:55.946
and are doing some
really good things.

00:36:55.946 --> 00:36:58.215 align:start position:0%
- What's the school that you're
working on here in Florida?

00:36:58.215 --> 00:37:00.384
Is it New School?
No, it's Florida-

00:37:00.384 --> 00:37:01.953 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That's right, the
New College of Florida.

00:37:01.953 --> 00:37:03.354
- New College. Sorry, yeah.

00:37:04.688 --> 00:37:06.557 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so New College of
Florida is a perfect example.

00:37:06.557 --> 00:37:09.493 align:start size:97% position:3%
Governor DeSantis appointed
a group of trustees,

00:37:09.493 --> 00:37:13.764
including me, to take over
this college, to reform it.

00:37:13.764 --> 00:37:16.300
To abolish their
DEI bureaucracy,

00:37:16.300 --> 00:37:18.802
to install principles
of colorblind equality,

00:37:18.802 --> 00:37:20.838 align:start size:84% position:16%
to hire new faculty
with expertise

00:37:20.838 --> 00:37:22.206
in the classical liberal arts,

00:37:22.206 --> 00:37:24.208 align:start size:88% position:13%
and to give the college
a renewed mission

00:37:24.208 --> 00:37:28.379
of pursuing the classical
liberal arts education,

00:37:28.379 --> 00:37:31.649 align:start size:97% position:3%
which kind of
paradoxically, is now coded

00:37:31.649 --> 00:37:33.985
as a Conservative education
because it's really, been-

00:37:33.985 --> 00:37:35.853 align:start size:81% position:19%
- It's my life
story right there.

00:37:35.853 --> 00:37:39.823 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Disappeared from all
of our institutions

00:37:39.823 --> 00:37:41.825
in the last few decades.

00:37:41.825 --> 00:37:44.729
And so look, this
is gonna be a home

00:37:44.729 --> 00:37:46.997
for a wide diversity of opinion,

00:37:46.997 --> 00:37:50.367
and we're actively
recruiting scholars

00:37:50.367 --> 00:37:53.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
aligned with the
classical liberal mission.

00:37:53.804 --> 00:37:57.174 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so we're hoping to
announce some new professors.

00:37:57.174 --> 00:37:59.075
I think some of the
people that maybe,

00:37:59.075 --> 00:38:02.379
you might be excited about,
are currently in the mix

00:38:02.379 --> 00:38:05.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
for the professorships
at the New College.

00:38:05.783 --> 00:38:09.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we're going to be
transforming this institution,

00:38:09.386 --> 00:38:12.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is not just important
for the campus itself,

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:14.391
but it's important
because it's a model

00:38:14.391 --> 00:38:17.995
for all other red state
governors and legislators

00:38:17.995 --> 00:38:21.565
and citizens to say, you
don't have to put up with,

00:38:21.565 --> 00:38:23.533
you know, "America's
Cultural Revolution."

00:38:23.533 --> 00:38:26.103 align:start size:97% position:3%
You don't have to put up
with all of your institution

00:38:26.103 --> 00:38:29.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
being captured by
left-wing ideologues.

00:38:29.006 --> 00:38:32.576
There is now a playbook for
retaking the institutions,

00:38:32.576 --> 00:38:34.211
reforming the institutions,

00:38:34.211 --> 00:38:37.148 align:start position:0%
and reorienting the institutions
towards your values.

00:38:37.948 --> 00:38:40.083
- I got one more for you, Rufo.

00:38:40.083 --> 00:38:42.519
We're posting this same week
that the book is coming out.

00:38:42.519 --> 00:38:45.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have no doubt that it
is gonna do extremely well

00:38:45.155 --> 00:38:46.657
and that it will
also be left off

00:38:46.657 --> 00:38:48.525
the New York Times Best Seller,

00:38:48.525 --> 00:38:50.394
I hate to tell you,
that's how it works.

00:38:50.394 --> 00:38:52.796 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I wonder, just in
terms of media strategy,

00:38:52.796 --> 00:38:55.465
I know you're willing to
go into the lions' den

00:38:55.465 --> 00:38:57.667
and go after these guys.

00:38:57.667 --> 00:39:02.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you willing to go
into MSNBC and go to CNN,

00:39:02.439 --> 00:39:06.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
and go to, say the "New York
Times" board over there,

00:39:06.310 --> 00:39:07.278
and all of those things?

00:39:07.278 --> 00:39:08.512
You're not gonna get the invites

00:39:08.512 --> 00:39:10.114
and they're also not
gonna respond to you,

00:39:10.114 --> 00:39:14.285
but just for the record, so
that once it's all out there.

00:39:14.285 --> 00:39:16.854 align:start size:88% position:13%
- (laughs) Yeah, of
course I am, I love it.

00:39:16.854 --> 00:39:19.623
I've not only stated
publicly, on Twitter,

00:39:19.623 --> 00:39:23.093 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I'm open to
interviews and stories

00:39:23.093 --> 00:39:26.831
and media appearances
with all of my critics,

00:39:26.831 --> 00:39:28.465
but I've actually reached out,

00:39:28.465 --> 00:39:30.634
my team has reached
out proactively to
all of those outlets.

00:39:30.634 --> 00:39:33.503
And I will say, and this
is a point of optimism,

00:39:33.503 --> 00:39:35.339
I think you'll be
happy about this,

00:39:35.339 --> 00:39:37.474
I'm sensing a bit of a shift.

00:39:37.474 --> 00:39:41.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've actually got a lot
of response from people at

00:39:41.178 --> 00:39:43.881
everywhere, from
"Jacobin" to MSNBC,

00:39:43.881 --> 00:39:45.883 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the "New York Times,"
to the "New Yorker."

00:39:45.883 --> 00:39:47.685
They're starting to reengage.

00:39:47.685 --> 00:39:48.919
And so I think you'll see-

00:39:48.919 --> 00:39:50.421
- But are they putting you on?

00:39:50.421 --> 00:39:53.056
Are you gonna be on MSNBC
to talk about this book?

00:39:53.056 --> 00:39:56.160
- MSNBC is the one that has
not responded unfortunately,

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:58.128
but a lot of the print
publications on the left

00:39:58.128 --> 00:40:00.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
have responded, they might
engage with interviews,

00:40:00.965 --> 00:40:02.633
they might engage with podcasts,

00:40:02.633 --> 00:40:04.467
they might engage with
reviews of the book.

00:40:04.467 --> 00:40:09.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so I really hope that
the book, which look,

00:40:09.006 --> 00:40:11.141 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think, a lot of
center-left audiences

00:40:11.141 --> 00:40:12.643
are gonna read this
and say, "oh wow,

00:40:12.643 --> 00:40:15.713
I heard that Rufo is a, you
know, right-wing fanatic,

00:40:15.713 --> 00:40:17.781 align:start size:84% position:16%
but actually, his
book is totally right.

00:40:17.781 --> 00:40:19.216
I agree with it."

00:40:19.216 --> 00:40:22.886 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think we might see the
beginnings of a reengagement,

00:40:22.886 --> 00:40:24.921
now that the kind of
George Floyd hysteria

00:40:24.921 --> 00:40:26.490
has started to die down.

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:29.259
I sense that the left is
maybe willing to start

00:40:29.259 --> 00:40:31.995 align:start size:97% position:3%
coming back to the table and
start engaging in debate.

00:40:31.995 --> 00:40:36.367
I hope that the book launches
that, accelerates that trend.

00:40:36.367 --> 00:40:39.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
I hope that not only
Conservative readers
appreciate it,

00:40:39.670 --> 00:40:43.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
but also center and
even center-left
readers appreciate it.

00:40:43.808 --> 00:40:46.076
I really want it
to be a focal point

00:40:46.076 --> 00:40:48.512
for reengaging in
this conversation.

00:40:48.512 --> 00:40:50.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
At the end of the day,
we're one country.

00:40:50.614 --> 00:40:51.849
We all have to live here,

00:40:51.849 --> 00:40:53.417
and we'll all be much better off

00:40:53.417 --> 00:40:55.586 align:start size:97% position:3%
if we can engage substantively
with one another.

00:40:55.586 --> 00:40:58.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I hope this book
contributes to that process.

00:40:58.622 --> 00:41:01.858 align:start size:88% position:13%
- The book is "America's
Cultural Revolution."

00:41:01.858 --> 00:41:04.128
And if they come, look,
you've even got a copy.

00:41:04.128 --> 00:41:06.463 align:start size:88% position:13%
You truly are a
professional, my friend.

00:41:06.463 --> 00:41:07.965
If they come for
you in Washington,

